  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1244 AM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nMostly Cloudy, Precipitation Chances Graze Forecast Area:\n\nA persistent forecast expected into Tuesday with shower potential \ngrazing western peripheral counties from southeast Minnesota into \ncentral Wisconsin. Upper level water vapor imagery and GOES derived \nupper level winds depict local ridging over the Upper Mississippi \nRiver Valley, causing a shield of limited returns on mosaic RADAR \nimagery across the local forecast area. While RADAR imagery loops \nsuggest the higher value reflectivity returns advecting towards the \nlocal forecast area overnight, a more meridional aspect to the \nsynoptic pattern (i.e., ridging) expected to limit local measurable \nrainfall coverage and amounts. \n\nThe area of low level cyclonic rotation will be delaying inevitable \ncyclolysis as it approaches the forecast area, limiting local \ncoverage in highest precipitation confidence Monday night into \nTuesday. Have slightly tempered coverage of highest PoPs as a result \nbut will be a close forecast detail to monitor even though overall \nimpact is limited.\n\nWednesday Night Preciptiation Chances:\n\nThe low confidence and low impact pattern continues through the week \nand into the weekend as the upper level low responsible for the \nhigher precipitation chances well upstream stalls over the Northern \nPlains before joining the low over the West Coast and eventually \nadvecting east through the Mississippi River Valley midweek. While \ninter-LREF member confidence has increased for measurable \nprecipitation overnight Wednesday into Thursday, intra-member and \nprobabilistic/deterministic spread is curious specifically within the \nEPS. \n\nAppears amount of local moisture remains the discerning factor due \nto the local forecast area being on the northern periphery of \nincreased low level moisture transport through the start of the week \nas well as a subsequent, well-upstream weak low level perturbation \nrefining direction of moisture advection towards the Upper \nMississippi River Valley. The increased moisture solutions \n(EPS/ECMWF 25.00/25.12Z) results in 0.5\"+ of rainfall primarily for \nlocations west of the Mississippi River Valley locally while most \nother solutions abstain rainfall amounts to 0.1\"-0.2\". Above \nfreezing surface temperatures keep overall impacts minimal although \nsoils could benefit from the quenching saturation. The frontal \nboundary and accompanying moisture will be on a weakening, \ndecreasing trend, lowering confidence for precipitation along and \neast of the Mississippi River Valley. Although, recent LREF trends \nhave been increasing certainty for measurable precipitation across \nthe forecast area through Thursday morning.\n\nWeekend Precipitation ChanceS:\n\nConfidence in weekend precipitation chances weakened compare to \nprevious forecasts, resulting in a decrease of NBM PoPs by 10-25%, \nnearing 60% in spots. An appendage to the synoptic anticyclonic flow \nover the southeastern U.S. expected to spread west and north as LREF \nmembers and their deterministic counterparts suggest some semblance \nof cyclogenesis over the central CONUS. Slight role reversal between \nthe EPS and GEFS over the last 24 hours with the EPS now suggesting \na stronger low into early next week.",
